# `text=auto` alone was already doing half the job: it normalises to LF on
# commit, which is why every blob in this repo is clean LF and none is mixed.
#
# What it does NOT do is pin the WORKING TREE. With no `eol`, checkout falls
# back to core.eol, which defaults to `native` - CRLF on Windows - and that is
# independent of core.autocrlf, so turning autocrlf off does not help either.
# The result was 55 CRLF / 48 LF on disk in the same checkout: every file that
# a tool had rewritten since checkout was LF, the rest CRLF.
#
# That split is how files end up with mixed endings INSIDE them, which
# silently breaks multi-line edits - a literal match picks one style and
# misses the other. The three consumer repos (admin, web, reader) hit exactly
# that and were pinned to LF on 2026-08-26; this is the same fix here, so a
# fresh clone on any machine agrees with them instead of depending on local
# git config.
#
# No .bat/.cmd/.ps1/.sh files are tracked here. Add an `eol=crlf` exception
# before committing one - the reader repo carries that carve-out for
# android/gradlew.bat.

* text=auto eol=lf
